{
  "dom_id": "step-q165416",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$",
  "worked_solution": "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }$, where $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta })^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta })^{2}+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{(-1)}{2}-(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
